1
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,200
Opens in a browser. No client to install.

2
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,000
The database ships with it and initialises on first run.

3
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,600
Chinese, English and Vietnamese.

4
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,600
Two builds: a self-contained Windows package, and a domestic-stack package.

5
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,100
Three steps to deploy: unpack, start, open a browser.

6
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:19,300
The data directory is configurable. Upgrades replace the program, not the data.

7
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,000
The device tree groups by company, workshop, then process.

8
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,100
Six devices and 262 variables, connected now.

9
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,100
Open a device and all 79 of its variables are listed.

10
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:48,600
The data comes from a Siemens S7-1200, on a one-second cycle.

11
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,600
Every variable carries a full semantic path.

12
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:03,800
Types: boolean, integer, float, double word, string.

13
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,600
The polling cycle is set per device.

14
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,600
A single device can be started or stopped.

15
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,600
Variables can be edited, exported as CSV, or a whole device copied.

16
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,800
Changes apply immediately. The collector does not restart.

17
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,300
For many tags, prepare them in Excel and import once.

18
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,200
The imported CSV goes straight into process analysis.

19
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,700
An existing Excel tag list imports in one go.

20
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,900
Same interface. Changing the protocol only changes the driver.

21
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:06,200
This aging furnace speaks Mitsubishi MC, with 35 variables.

22
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:14,500
Zone one reads 100.2 degrees, a D-register address, unsigned 16-bit.

23
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,700
Address, type and cycle are parsed by the driver's own rules.

24
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:27,800
S7 data blocks, Mitsubishi D and M areas, Modbus registers.

25
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:34,000
The differences are absorbed in the driver. Above it, every variable looks the same.

26
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,600
Thirty-one acquisition drivers and eleven database drivers built in.

27
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,900
Seven categories covering the main industrial protocols.

28
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:58,400
Modbus, Siemens, Mitsubishi, OPC UA, HTTP, MQTT.

29
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,700
A protocol you do not have can be uploaded as a plugin and hot-loaded.

30
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:17,900
Four database drivers: MySQL, SQL Server, PostgreSQL, Dameng.

31
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,800
Plugins can be written in-house or supplied by a vendor.

32
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,100
Every upload is recorded: source and install time.

33
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:43,600
General category: HTTP REST, OPC DA, SECS-GEM, SNMP, OPC UA.

34
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,900
Add a device: category, protocol, then address and port.

35
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,600
The parameter panel switches with the protocol.

36
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:04,100
Tags can be added one by one, imported as CSV, or copied from another device.

37
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,900
Devices on another subnet go through a tunnel.

38
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,600
No need to open a port for every device.

39
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,500
Semantic management: organise tags into an ontology.

40
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,900
Devices, process steps, relations, metrics.

41
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Tags map to the ontology, and the ontology is maintained by you.

42
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,600
Semantics are what give the data business meaning.

43
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:39,900
Workshops name things differently; the semantic layer unifies them.

44
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Only then does a cross-workshop comparison mean anything.

45
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:51,400
Mapping: the raw tag label to an ontology attribute.

46
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:02,600
Bindings, covered attributes, key bindings, relation instances, inheritance.

47
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Candidate bindings are confirmed or withdrawn one by one.

48
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:20,500
Renaming a device changes the mapping only. History is not recomputed.

49
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:27,200
Unconfirmed candidates wait as pending. They never apply silently.

50
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:38,200
Relation layer: synonym, cross-reference, upstream, key, schedule, order, acquisition.

51
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:53,800
Nodes: workshop, line, device, variable, data source, binding.

52
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:05,000
A 3D view: structure, in space.

53
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:11,100
At a glance, how many tags each machine carries.

54
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:16,800
Every relation carries a count. These are real references.

55
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:23,600
Drag with the left button and the graph turns.

56
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,600
Turn it and the device clusters separate.

57
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:33,300
Extrusion, melting and aging each form their own cluster.

58
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,700
Variable nodes hang off a device like branches.

59
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,900
You cannot see this structure by paging through tables.

60
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,900
Turn it round and the variables under a device come into view.

61
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:53,100
Dozens of tags per machine, and the count is visible.

62
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:59,300
Scroll to zoom: in for a detail, out for the whole.

63
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:04,600
One button restores the view.

64
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:13,800
The diagnostic path: plant, workshop, device, variable.

65
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:18,400
"Whole view" resets the camera. It is not an undo.

66
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:25,600
Force layout: it settles by relation.

67
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,600
Tightly related machines pull together.

68
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:38,400
Add a device or change the semantics and the graph changes at once.

69
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:44,100
The more devices, the more the line structure shows.

70
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,700
What drifts to the edge is usually an orphan variable.

71
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,600
Which is exactly how you find the gaps.

72
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:02,800
Stellar view: nodes over a sphere, edges woven across it.

73
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:07,800
Rotate to read density, not a single point.

74
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:14,500
With thousands of tags, dense and empty areas are obvious.

75
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:22,700
It shows, visually, how much scale the system carries.

76
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:27,200
Uneven density shows which processes carry most points.

77
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:33,100
Industry templates ship with aluminium and other scenarios.

78
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:39,900
Device models, tag naming and metric definitions are already there.

79
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:44,300
A new project starts from a template, not from zero.

80
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:50,400
Applying one generates the ontology nodes and mapping rules.

81
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:56,500
Across parallel projects, the template is maintained once.

82
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:08,600
Hermes: an assistant that knows the devices, the variables and the semantic paths.

83
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:17,800
Ask it directly: status, variable comparison, connection faults.

84
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,600
Scheduled tasks produce reports on time.

85
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:30,000
Documents: feed the assistant your site material.

86
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,900
An analysis done three times can be fixed as a skill.

87
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:40,400
Next time you call it. You do not re-describe it.

88
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,900
A real question: summarise device status.

89
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:51,900
It does not invent an answer. It decomposes the task first.

90
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:59,600
Five steps: list devices, count online, total variables, check details, conclude.

91
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:07,600
Every step calls a real platform tool.

92
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,600
Each step's time and result are recorded.

93
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:17,600
A failure can be located to the step.

94
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:24,000
The task list shows the step, the current action, and what is done.

95
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,600
You can tell whether it is working or stuck.

96
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:40,000
The plan lives on the server. Log in elsewhere and it continues.

97
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:46,900
It has a process. It can be interrupted. It can be inspected.

98
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:50,600
It can do jobs like "check every device".

99
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:57,200
Not a one-shot answer, but an observable execution.

100
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:04,600
A second AI product: the live board for press 27.

101
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:14,700
A conclusion, four metric cards, and the schedule detail.

102
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:23,400
Recipe, ram speed and schedule all come from the database.

103
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:29,600
The live area refreshes every three seconds.

104
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:36,600
Built for a shop-floor screen, sized to be read from a distance.

105
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:45,200
Left: measured ram speed. Each square wave is one cycle.

106
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:52,200
Right: the set speed. The shift shows a setting was changed.

107
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:00,700
Side by side, plan against actual is visible at a glance.

108
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:09,300
Tens of thousands of points at one second each: equal widths mean it is right.

109
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:36,300
Metadata, six metric cards, a conclusion, and SVG charts.

110
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:44,400
Totals are computed by the framework. The page self-checks consistency.

111
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:50,700
The header states the rule version, the base time and the data source.

112
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:57,600
A deliverable you can send to the workshop.

113
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:03,200
Each row carries the plan and work-order number, so it reconciles to MES.

114
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,900
No manual cross-checking.

115
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:13,900
Data source management: write collected data into the business database.

116
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:19,400
MySQL, SQL Server, PostgreSQL, Dameng.

117
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:28,900
One table takes many variables, incrementally or in full.

118
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:32,900
A target can be disabled at any time without affecting collection.

119
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:34,900
The write policy is configured per table.

120
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:38,900
Different tables can use different cycles and field mappings.

121
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:42,600
The system database supports the same four.

122
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:49,100
Dameng loads its driver on demand; connection string and schema adapt.

123
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:59,900
If the service is down it says so, and retries every ten seconds.

124
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,900
It will not start hard into an error.

125
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:10,600
The connection is verified before switching.

126
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:15,700
If verification fails, the current configuration is untouched.

127
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:22,600
Six devices, 262 variables, one process.

128
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:34,200
The closing card is from an earlier build and counts installed plugins too. The current release ships 30 acquisition drivers built in.
